# Enrich policy for the ENRICH variant of the consolidated rule
# (10008_single_stage_enrich_variant).
#
# Run this on the LOCAL / CCS cluster -- the same cluster Kibana runs on. Nothing
# is needed on prod: the rule uses ENRICH _coordinator:, which forces the enrich
# to execute on the coordinating (local) cluster, so the policy only has to exist
# here.
#
# PREREQUISITE: kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich must already exist (keyword-mapped,
# populated) on this cluster. Create it with kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich_index. This
# file no longer creates its own throwaway reindex source -- kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich
# is now the single source of truth you write to directly, so there is nothing
# recurring to reindex.
#
# Why not build the policy straight on kibana_sdp_amdb?
# ----------------------------------------------------
# Because enrich cannot match on kibana_sdp_amdb's hostname. That field is `text`
# with a `.keyword` multi-field, and enrich's match_field validation only walks
# `object` fields (EnrichPolicyRunner.validateAndGetMappingTypeAndFormat), so it
# throws "The [hostname] field must be regular object but was [text]". Nor can it
# match the bare text field -- enrich matches with a TermQuery
# (MatchProcessor.getQueryBuilder), which never matches an analyzed hostname
# whole. kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich fixes this by mapping hostname as plain keyword.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The policy, on the keyword source of truth
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# match_field is the top-level keyword `hostname`. The rule matches it against
# host.hostname from metricbeat (already keyword), so the types line up.
# enrich_fields is just the two fields the rule filters on -- enrich adds its
# fields as new columns and same-named columns get overwritten, so keeping the
# list narrow avoids shadowing a metricbeat column.
PUT _enrich/policy/kibana_sdp_amdb_policy
{
  "match": {
    "indices": "kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich",
    "match_field": "hostname",
    "enrich_fields": [
      "group",
      "alert_status"
    ]
  }
}

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Execute
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
PUT _enrich/policy/kibana_sdp_amdb_policy/_execute

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keeping it current -- the one thing the source-of-truth model does NOT remove
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An enrich policy is a POINT-IN-TIME SNAPSHOT. _execute REINDEXES the source into
# a separate hidden .enrich-* index (EnrichPolicyRunner, ~lines 479-508), and the
# ENRICH command reads THAT, never the live source. So a record you add to or
# delete from kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich has NO effect on the rule until you re-run:
#
#   PUT _enrich/policy/kibana_sdp_amdb_policy/_execute
#
# Config changes rarely, so hourly/daily is plenty -- but wire the _execute into
# whatever maintains kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich, or schedule it; do not leave it
# manual. Until you do, newly enabled hosts will not alert and newly disabled
# hosts will keep alerting.
#
# This snapshot behaviour is inherent to enrich. The only way to get truly-live
# config (writes take effect immediately, no _execute) is the LOOKUP JOIN route
# against the same kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich index -- see 10008_single_stage_enrich_index
# -- which reads the index live. That route needs the index on prod instead.

# Inspect / clean up:
GET _enrich/policy/kibana_sdp_amdb_policy
GET _enrich/_stats
#   DELETE _enrich/policy/kibana_sdp_amdb_policy    # delete the policy before its source index
